## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to architect true cross-project isolation with scalable tenant onboarding, plus governance controls across storage, analytics, and metadata systems.

## Key Concepts
- IAM conditions and per-tenant service accounts
- Tenant-specific data planes (GCS prefixes, BigQuery datasets)
- Private Service Connect and VPC Service Controls for restricted egress
- Data Catalog/Dataplex as a data governance layer
- Dataflow/Test hooks for ingestion, auditing, rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-config: per-tenant IAM condition binding (conceptual)
{
  "binding": {
    "members": ["serviceAccount:tenant-123@project.iam.gserviceaccount.com"],
    "role": "roles/bigquery.dataViewer",
    "condition": {
      "title": "Tenant-specific dataset access",
      "expression": "resource.name.startsWith('projects/_/datasets/tenant_123_')"
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests familiarity with GCP primitives for multi-tenant secrets, security isolation, API design, and basic caching/observability, suitable for a beginner level.

## Key Concepts

- Secret Manager with per-tenant secrets and IAM isolation
- Cloud Run security and API key authentication
- Lightweight cache to reduce Secret Manager calls (60s)
- Observability via Cloud Logging and Monitoring

## Code Example

```javascript
const {SecretManagerServiceClient} = require('@google-cloud/secret-manager');
const client = new SecretManagerServiceClient();

async function accessSecretVersion(name) {
  const [version] = await client.accessSecretVersion({ name });
  return version.payload.data.toString('utf8');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you rotate API keys and enforce revocation?
- How would you validate secret updates and cache invalidation?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world multi-tenant data pipelines demand strong isolation, auditable control planes, and robust failure handling. This question probes identity management, data residency, and operational resilience in GCP.

## Key Concepts
- Partner-signed URLs for secure ingestion
- Cloud Functions validation and Pub/Sub as a decoupler
- Dataflow (streaming/batch) with per-tenant partitioning
- CMEK and tenant-scoped IAM + Workload Identity Federation
- VPC Service Controls perimeter to prevent exfiltration
- Idempotent processing and replay protection
- Rollback and test strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: publish validated message to Pub/Sub from a Cloud Function (Node.js)
const {PubSub} = require('@google-cloud/pubsub');
const pubsub = new PubSub();
exports.validateAndPublish = async (event) => {
  const record = JSON.parse(event.body);
  // basic schema check
  if (!record.tenant_id || !record.file_uri) throw new Error('Invalid');
  await pubsub.topic('tenant-ingest').publish(Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(record)));
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate per-tenant data isolation in BigQuery? 
- How would you test handling of schema evolution and data drift?
